+# Tool Input Validation
+
+| Field  | Value                |
+| ------ | -------------------- |
+| Issue  | re-cinq/HALEngine#37 |
+| Status | In Progress          |
+
+Every tool declares a JSON Schema `inputSchema` (`src/types/ai.ts`) that is handed verbatim to both providers, and `ToolRegistry.execute` (`src/orchestration/tools/registry.ts`) calls the executor without ever checking the model's input against it, leaving every consumer to validate its own input differently or not at all. This spec states the contract the registry owns from here on: the registry, not each tool's executor, rejects input the declared schema refuses, before the executor runs, and a rejection reaches the model as something it can read and correct within the existing tool-round budget. Validation is unconditional — there is no opt-out.
+
+## The contract
+
+- `execute` validates `input` against the resolved definition's `inputSchema` before the executor runs; input the schema refuses returns a `ToolResponse` whose `result` names the failure, and the executor never runs ([validated by: rejects input the schema refuses before the executor runs](../../src/orchestration/tools/registry.acceptance.test.ts#L63)).
+- Input the schema accepts reaches the executor unchanged, so a property the schema does not mention passes through with the rest — no injected default, no type coercion, no dropped key ([validated by: hands valid input to the executor unchanged, unlisted properties included, exactly once](../../src/orchestration/tools/registry.acceptance.test.ts#L76)).
+- A wrong-typed field is rejected with a message naming the property path and the expected type, and the message never carries the offending value ([validated by: rejects a wrong-typed field naming the path and the expected type, never the value](../../src/orchestration/tools/registry.acceptance.test.ts#L86)).
+- `register` compiles a static tool's schema at registration, so a schema no JSON Schema validator accepts throws there, naming the tool, rather than mid-conversation ([validated by: refuses an uncompilable schema at register time, naming the tool](../../src/orchestration/tools/registry.acceptance.test.ts#L100)).
+- A function-valued `ToolDefinitionSource` is resolved per call, so each call is validated against the schema current at that call, never against a schema cached from an earlier one ([validated by: validates a function-valued definition against the schema current at each call](../../src/orchestration/tools/registry.acceptance.test.ts#L121)).
+- A static tool's schema is compiled once at registration: no later edit to the schema object reaches the validator, across any number of `execute` calls ([validated by: validates ten execute calls of a static tool against the schema compiled at register](../../src/orchestration/tools/registry.acceptance.test.ts#L137)).
+- A rejection is returned to the model as the tool result of its round — it does not throw — so the same conversation can retry with corrected input and still complete ([validated by: returns a schema rejection to the model and completes the message on the corrected retry](../../src/orchestration/tools/registry.acceptance.test.ts#L149)).
+- `execute` still throws `Unknown tool: get_weather` for a name nothing registered claims; that failure class stays a throw, handled the opposite way on purpose ([validated by: still throws Unknown tool: get_weather for an unregistered name](../../src/orchestration/tools/registry.acceptance.test.ts#L179)).
+- No rejection message and no log line carries a property value: both name property paths and the constraint that failed, because tool input is where identifying data enters the engine and the engine redacts nothing ([validated by: keeps an identifying value out of the rejection text and the log lines](../../src/orchestration/tools/registry.acceptance.test.ts#L192)).
+
+## The documented contract
+
+- `README.md` and `docs/adding-a-tool.md` state that input reaching an executor has already passed the declared schema, that unknown keys may still be present, and that a rejection is returned to the model rather than thrown, so an executor no longer casts defensively or hand-validates its input.
+- `.specify/spec.md` records that a rejection is a tool result rather than an exception, that the tool-round bound is what stops a rejected call from spinning into an unbounded retry cycle, and that extra properties pass through unmodified.
+
+## Out of scope
+
+- Validating tool output; hallucination detection; an evaluation harness.
+- Per-tool authorization: a schema-valid call for someone else's data is still valid — authorization is a `ToolContext` boundary concern owned by no issue in this epic.
+- Retry, timeout and rate limiting around tool execution, and any actual tool implementation, which is written against this contract later.
+- The `Unknown tool` throw becoming a returned result instead, which is tracked as its own issue.
